    "result_text": "All tests pass. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI made all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, while keeping the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\n**Root cause:** Several escalation handlers responded with ad-hoc bodies \u2014 `{\"errors\":[...]}` (an `Errors` record) for the field/conflict rejections and a plain string for the daily-limit case \u2014 all serialized as `application/json`. The RFC7807 infrastructure (`ProblemDetails` + `ProblemDetail`) already existed and was used by a few handlers, but even those didn't advertise the `problem+json` media type, because OfficeFloor's `ObjectResponse` defaults to `application/json`.\n\n**Changes** (`src/main/java/.../rest/escalation/`):\n\n- **`ProblemDetails.java`** \u2014 added a `response(ex, status, detail)` helper that wraps the built `ProblemDetail` in a `ResponseEntity` carrying `Content-Type: application/problem+json`, so the media type is emitted regardless of the request's `Accept` header.\n- Converted the eight rejection handlers to `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` via that helper, using each exception's descriptive `getMessage()` as the `detail`:\n  - **400:** `MissingOwnerFields`, `DisposableEmailDomain`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`\n  - **409:** `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n  - **429:** `DailyLimitExceeded`\n- **`ValidationExceptionHandler.java`** (400) \u2014 already built a `ProblemDetail` with `schemaValidationErrors`; added the `application/problem+json` content type so it too advertises the correct media type.\n\nEach body carries all four required members: `type` (`about:blank`), `title` (exception simple name), `status`, and `detail`.\n\nI left the non-rejection handlers (403/404/500) untouched, since the change is scoped to 400/409/429.",
